29. Laplace Transforms
29.1. Definition of the Laplace Transform One-dimensional Laplace Transform
(f(s)=\mathcal{L}^{\infty}\left\{F(t)\right\}=\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-st}F(t)dt\]
F(t) is a function of the real variable \(t\) and \(s\) is a complex variable. \(F(t)\) is called the original function and f(s) is called the image function. If the integral in 29.1.1 converges for a real s=\delta_{0}\) i.e.,

exists, then it converges for all \(s\) with \(\mathcal{R}s>\delta_{0}\), and the image function is a single valued analytic function of \(s\) in the half-plane \(\mathcal{R}s>s_{0}\): Two-dimensional Laplace Transform
(f(u,v)=\mathcal{L}^{\infty}\left\{F(x,y)\right\}=\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-ux-vF}(x,y)dxdy\)
